steps.body:
An **abstract class** is a parent that only exists to be extended. Mark it `abstract` and it can declare **abstract methods**: a signature with no body, ending in a semicolon. Each subclass fills them in.

```java
abstract class Shape {
    private final String name;

    Shape(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    abstract double area();   // no body

    String describe() {       // concrete, calls the abstract one
        return name + " " + area();
    }
}
```

Without `abstract`, `Shape` would need a fake `double area() { return 0; }` that no real shape should ever run. An abstract class can still hold fields, constructors, and finished methods. Here `describe()` is written once and works for every shape, because `area()` runs the subclass version.

From here on, code leaves `public` off the methods and constructors of helper classes. In a one-file program that changes nothing, since every class shares a package. Fields stay `private`, and an override of a `public` method, like `toString`, still needs `public`.

The rules the compiler enforces:

- `new Shape("blob")` is a compile error: an abstract class cannot be instantiated.
- Its constructor still runs, through `super(...)` in each subclass.
- A subclass must override every abstract method, or be declared `abstract` itself.
- A `Shape` variable can hold any concrete subclass: `Shape s = new Circle(2.0);`

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape circle = new Circle(2.0);
        Shape rectangle = new Rectangle(3.0, 4.5);
        System.out.println(circle.describe());
        System.out.println(rectangle.describe());
        // Shape blob = new Shape("Blob");  // compile error: Shape is abstract
    }
}

abstract class Shape {
    private final String name;

    Shape(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    abstract double area();

    String describe() {
        return String.format("%s with area %.2f", name, area());
    }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    private final double radius;

    Circle(double radius) {
        super("Circle");
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override
    double area() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

class Rectangle extends Shape {
    private final double width;
    private final double height;

    Rectangle(double width, double height) {
        super("Rectangle");
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    @Override
    double area() {
        return width * height;
    }
}

