steps.body:
An **interface** is a pure contract: a list of methods a class promises to have. A class signs it with `implements`.

```java
interface Drawable {
    String draw();                  // public and abstract by default

    default String framed() {       // a default method has a body
        return "[" + draw() + "]";
    }
}

class Square implements Drawable, Resizable {
    @Override
    public String draw() { ... }
}
```

An interface's abstract and default methods are `public` even when you leave the word out, and an override cannot be less visible. So the implementation must say `public` too. Leave it out and you get a compile error about weaker access privileges, the most common interface mistake.

- A class extends one class but can implement **several interfaces**, separated by commas.
- A **default method** has a body that every implementer inherits. A class may still override it.
- An interface has no constructors and no instance fields. A field you write in one is a `static final` constant. Need per-object state or a constructor? Use an abstract class.
- An interface is a type. `List<Drawable>` can hold a `Square`, a `Label`, or anything else that implements `Drawable`, and you call only `Drawable` methods on its items.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Square small = new Square(2);
        Square big = new Square(3);
        Resizable resizable = big;
        resizable.scale(2);

        List<Drawable> items = new ArrayList<>();
        items.add(small);
        items.add(big);
        items.add(new Label("Exit"));

        for (Drawable item : items) {
            System.out.println(item.framed());
        }
    }
}

interface Drawable {
    String draw();

    default String framed() {
        return "[" + draw() + "]";
    }
}

interface Resizable {
    void scale(int factor);
}

class Square implements Drawable, Resizable {
    private int side;

    Square(int side) {
        this.side = side;
    }

    @Override
    public String draw() {
        return "Square " + side + "x" + side;
    }

    @Override
    public void scale(int factor) {
        side *= factor;
    }
}

class Label implements Drawable {
    private final String text;

    Label(String text) {
        this.text = text;
    }

    @Override
    public String draw() {
        return "Label " + text;
    }

    @Override
    public String framed() {
        return "<" + draw() + ">";
    }
}

