import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Song first = new Song("Morning Light", "The Tides", 5);
        Song second = new Song("Paper Moon", "Ada Reyes", 4);

        List<Playable> playlist = new ArrayList<>();
        playlist.add(first);
        playlist.add(second);
        for (Playable track : playlist) {
            System.out.println(track.announce());
        }

        Ratable best = first;
        System.out.println("Top rating: " + best.rating());
    }
}

interface Playable {
    String play();

    default String announce() {
        return "Now playing: " + play();
    }
}

interface Ratable {
    int rating();
}

class Song implements Playable, Ratable {
    private final String title;
    private final String artist;
    private final int stars;

    Song(String title, String artist, int stars) {
        this.title = title;
        this.artist = artist;
        this.stars = stars;
    }

    @Override
    public String play() {
        return title + " by " + artist;
    }

    @Override
    public int rating() {
        return stars;
    }
}

steps.body:
Put mixed objects in a list typed by their abstract class or interface and loop once. Each call runs the method of the object's real class, chosen at run time. That is **dynamic dispatch**, and it is why you rarely need to ask what type something is.

When you do need a subclass-only method, the variable's type will not let you call it, as the Inheritance lesson showed. Say `s` is a `Shape` and only `Circle` has `getRadius()`. Check with `instanceof`, then **cast**:

```java
if (s instanceof Circle) {
    Circle c = (Circle) s;   // explicit cast
    System.out.println(c.getRadius());
}
```

The `s instanceof Circle c` shortcut is only a disabled preview feature in Java 15, a compile error here, so always cast on its own line after the check. `instanceof` is false for `null`, and casting to the wrong class throws `ClassCastException`, which is why the check comes first.

One trap: **overloading is picked at compile time, overriding at run time.** Given `print(Shape s)` and `print(Circle c)`, a call like `print(mystery)` where `Shape mystery = new Circle(1.0)` calls `print(Shape)`, because the compiler only sees the declared type `Shape`. Inside it, `mystery.area()` still runs the `Circle` override. Cast the argument to get the other overload.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Shape> shapes = new ArrayList<>();
        shapes.add(new Circle(1.0));
        shapes.add(new Rectangle(2.0, 3.0));
        shapes.add(new Circle(2.5));

        double total = 0;
        int circles = 0;
        for (Shape s : shapes) {
            total += s.area();
            if (s instanceof Circle) {
                Circle c = (Circle) s;
                circles++;
                System.out.printf("Circle with radius %.1f%n", c.getRadius());
            }
        }
        System.out.printf("Total area: %.2f%n", total);
        System.out.println("Circles: " + circles);

        Shape mystery = new Circle(1.0);
        print(mystery);
        print((Circle) mystery);
    }

    static void print(Shape s) {
        System.out.printf("print(Shape): area %.2f%n", s.area());
    }

    static void print(Circle c) {
        System.out.printf("print(Circle): radius %.1f%n", c.getRadius());
    }
}

abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    private final double radius;

    Circle(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    double getRadius() {
        return radius;
    }

    @Override
    double area() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

class Rectangle extends Shape {
    private final double width;
    private final double height;

    Rectangle(double width, double height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    @Override
    double area() {
        return width * height;
    }
}

